WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.540
A model that holds survival fixed can miss something.

2
00:00:07.820 --> 00:00:12.820
If inbreeding keeps building, survival or fertility can fall, and the trend tilts

3
00:00:12.820 --> 00:00:16.500
down. Can adding new genes tilt it back up?

4
00:00:17.450 --> 00:00:22.550
Southern Florida held a small, isolated population of Florida panthers.

5
00:00:23.850 --> 00:00:28.950
Its animals showed signs linked to inbreeding: kinked tails, heart defects and poor

6
00:00:28.950 --> 00:00:29.950
sperm.

7
00:00:30.483 --> 00:00:32.363
Put them on the first film's chart.

8
00:00:34.063 --> 00:00:35.163
Numbers sit close to the floor.

9
00:00:36.583 --> 00:00:40.023
Inbreeding can lower survival, so the gold arrow tilts down.

10
00:00:41.243 --> 00:00:42.363
The vortex is turning.

11
00:00:43.467 --> 00:00:47.227
In 1995, managers tried a genetic rescue.

12
00:00:48.527 --> 00:00:52.627
They released eight female pumas from Texas into the Florida population.

13
00:00:54.317 --> 00:00:58.737
Texas pumas belong to the same species, from a separate population.

14
00:01:00.137 --> 00:01:04.157
Adding animals to a population that still exists is called reinforcement.

15
00:01:05.697 --> 00:01:10.197
The aim was genetic: to reverse inbreeding by bringing in new gene versions.

16
00:01:11.367 --> 00:01:16.267
Over the following years, more and more panthers carried both Florida and Texas

17
00:01:16.267 --> 00:01:20.347
ancestry. Biologists call such animals admixed.

18
00:01:21.300 --> 00:01:23.700
To judge the rescue, track two things.

19
00:01:24.900 --> 00:01:25.920
One is the head count.

20
00:01:27.120 --> 00:01:32.620
The other is heterozygosity: the share of gene positions where an animal carries two

21
00:01:32.620 --> 00:01:33.620
different versions.

22
00:01:34.483 --> 00:01:38.863
In 2010, a team led by Warren Johnson reported the result.

23
00:01:40.623 --> 00:01:41.623
Numbers had about tripled.

24
00:01:43.123 --> 00:01:44.483
Heterozygosity had doubled.

25
00:01:46.283 --> 00:01:48.043
Survival and fitness measures had improved.

26
00:01:49.203 --> 00:01:51.103
On the chart, the arrow tilts up.

27
00:01:52.067 --> 00:01:57.187
The same report warned that habitat loss and persistent inbreeding remained

28
00:01:57.187 --> 00:02:02.127
concerns. So, if habitat keeps shrinking, will the climb continue?

29
00:02:08.733 --> 00:02:16.573
In 2024, a team led by Dave Onorato returned, with records from 1,192 panthers.

30
00:02:17.893 --> 00:02:20.933
The genetic benefits had lasted through five generations.

31
00:02:22.167 --> 00:02:27.767
Yet the population stabilised, and then declined, during 2016 to 2020.

32
00:02:29.087 --> 00:02:31.847
The genetic benefits held, yet the climb stopped.

33
00:02:33.127 --> 00:02:38.227
A population gain from genetic rescue may not last if other causes of decline

34
00:02:38.227 --> 00:02:39.227
remain.

35
00:02:39.800 --> 00:02:41.660
How did the teams judge the rescue?

36
00:02:42.900 --> 00:02:47.900
They compared admixed with non-admixed animals, and the years after the release with

37
00:02:47.900 --> 00:02:52.800
the years before. Both comparisons came from a single population.

38
00:02:53.933 --> 00:02:58.253
So genetic rescue can ease inbreeding and lift survival and births.

39
00:02:59.453 --> 00:03:04.833
But it does not by itself remove other causes, such as habitat loss or road deaths.

40
00:03:06.313 --> 00:03:08.173
Address those too, or the gain may fade.

41
00:03:09.613 --> 00:03:11.273
Genes and habitat work together.
